WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:05.664
When a customer texts your business, someone has to be given the conversation.

00:00:05.664 --> 00:00:09.995
This video shows how a queue hands out conversations, with a shared pool

00:00:09.995 --> 00:00:14.381
or with routing, and how an agent transfers a conversation to a colleague

00:00:14.381 --> 00:00:15.880
or back to the queue.

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:24.430
A text that lands in a shared list can be answered by anyone,

00:00:24.430 --> 00:00:27.850
which means that sometimes it is answered by no one.

00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:31.948
A busy team wants the text to reach a free person, and wants

00:00:31.948 --> 00:00:34.840
it to move on when that person does not pick it up.

00:00:35.590 --> 00:00:40.810
And a conversation sometimes needs another person, for a question that belongs to

00:00:40.810 --> 00:00:44.470
a different skill, without the customer starting again.

00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:52.664
An inbound entry point sends the texts of a number to an agent

00:00:52.664 --> 00:00:53.839
or to a queue.

00:00:53.839 --> 00:00:58.010
A text to an agent's personal number goes straight to that agent.

00:00:58.760 --> 00:01:00.969
A queue can use a shared pool.

00:01:00.969 --> 00:01:06.370
Every agent of the queue sees the conversation, and the first to claim it owns it.

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:09.957
A queue can also use routed distribution.

00:01:09.957 --> 00:01:14.725
The conversation is pushed to one available agent who has the inbox open,

00:01:14.725 --> 00:01:17.320
and who has the fewest conversations.

00:01:18.070 --> 00:01:22.106
If the agent does not pick it up within five minutes, it is

00:01:22.106 --> 00:01:23.969
routed to another agent.

00:01:23.969 --> 00:01:29.060
After two such moves, it falls back to the shared pool, so it is never lost.

00:01:33.254 --> 00:01:40.412
Open Interaction Center, Management, Inbound entry points, and edit the entry point of a number.

00:01:40.412 --> 00:01:45.254
Route to chooses a queue, a specific agent, or an AI assistant.

00:01:45.710 --> 00:01:48.546
Target queue is the queue it routes to.

00:01:48.546 --> 00:01:54.345
Queue distribution chooses between Shared pool, where agents claim to own, and Routed,

00:01:54.345 --> 00:01:58.000
where one available agent is given the conversation.

00:01:58.455 --> 00:02:03.860
In the inbox, the Available switch decides whether you accept routed conversations.

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:10.115
Routed distribution only gives conversations to agents who are available and have the inbox open.

00:02:14.307 --> 00:02:18.415
A conversation nobody holds must be claimed first.

00:02:18.415 --> 00:02:22.157
Open it and click Claim, then click Transfer.

00:02:22.608 --> 00:02:25.861
Choose A person, and pick a colleague from the list.

00:02:25.861 --> 00:02:30.628
The list offers only people who can use the messaging workspace, and never you.

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:35.171
Add a Note for the recipient, up to five hundred characters.

00:02:35.171 --> 00:02:40.440
The note stays in the history for other agents, and the customer never sees it.

00:02:40.896 --> 00:02:42.086
Click Transfer.

00:02:42.086 --> 00:02:46.222
It is the same conversation, so its whole history goes with it, and

00:02:46.222 --> 00:02:48.999
the thread says who transferred it to whom.

00:02:48.999 --> 00:02:50.926
The new owner gets a notice.

00:02:55.116 --> 00:02:59.097
Sometimes you cannot help, and nobody in particular should.

00:02:59.097 --> 00:03:03.446
Claim a conversation, click Transfer, and choose Back to a queue.

00:03:03.898 --> 00:03:07.816
Pick the queue, add a note if it helps, and click Transfer.

00:03:07.816 --> 00:03:12.998
The conversation goes back to that queue's shared inbox for any member to claim.

00:03:13.449 --> 00:03:17.219
Only the agent who holds a conversation can transfer it.

00:03:17.219 --> 00:03:22.449
A supervisor with permission to view all conversations can transfer any of them.

00:03:26.649 --> 00:03:31.651
A busy support team turns on routed distribution, so each new text goes

00:03:31.651 --> 00:03:34.379
to the agent with the lightest load.

00:03:35.129 --> 00:03:39.521
A frontline agent transfers a billing question to a colleague, and leaves a

00:03:39.521 --> 00:03:42.449
note so the customer does not have to repeat it.

00:03:43.199 --> 00:03:47.692
An agent going to lunch turns Available off, and sends the conversations they

00:03:47.692 --> 00:03:49.749
cannot finish back to the queue.

00:03:51.749 --> 00:03:56.057
Choose how a queue hands out conversations, and move a conversation to the

00:03:56.057 --> 00:03:58.049
right person when it needs one.

00:03:58.049 --> 00:03:59.449
Thanks for watching.

